WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.365
What your redactor misses.

2
00:00:01.645 --> 00:00:04.141
When the tests pass, list what still gets through.

3
00:00:04.421 --> 00:00:08.624
These are false negatives: sensitive data the redactor didn’t catch.

4
00:00:08.904 --> 00:00:11.443
Names, addresses, and free-text details.

5
00:00:11.723 --> 00:00:16.779
“This is Maria Lopez from the Portland office” has no pattern for a regex to find.

6
00:00:17.059 --> 00:00:22.861
Catching it needs named-entity recognition, and even then accuracy varies with language and domain.

7
00:00:23.141 --> 00:00:25.467
Obfuscated or reformatted values.

8
00:00:25.747 --> 00:00:33.640
“ana at example dot com”, a phone number split across a line break, digits written as words, or text in a non-Latin script.

9
00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:35.328
Formats you didn’t write.

10
00:00:35.608 --> 00:00:38.381
The phone pattern above knows North American and E.

11
00:00:38.661 --> 00:00:40.475
164 formats.

12
00:00:40.755 --> 00:00:50.867
A UK number written as 020 7946 0958 sails through, as do extensions and many national formats.

13
00:00:51.147 --> 00:00:53.707
Secrets without a recognizable prefix.

14
00:00:53.987 --> 00:01:03.373
Your pattern catches sk- keys (Anthropic’s admin keys, for instance, start with sk-ant-admin, per its Admin API docs) and bearer headers.

15
00:01:03.653 --> 00:01:13.787
A database password in a connection string, a random 32-character token, or a key inside a base64 blob gets through unless you add a rule for it.

16
00:01:14.067 --> 00:01:16.712
Sensitive meaning without sensitive tokens.

17
00:01:16.992 --> 00:01:22.624
“I was just diagnosed and can’t work this month” contains health information and no pattern at all.

18
00:01:22.904 --> 00:01:33.507
There are false positives too: a 10-digit order number matches the phone pattern, and over-eager rules turn logs into strings of placeholders that no one can debug from.

19
00:01:33.787 --> 00:01:35.152
Better tools exist.

20
00:01:35.432 --> 00:01:52.349
Presidio, an open-source toolkit for finding and anonymizing PII (originally from Microsoft, now community-maintained), combines named-entity models with regular expressions, rule-based logic, checksums, and surrounding context, in several languages.

21
00:01:52.629 --> 00:02:00.459
Its own documentation says there is no guarantee it will find all sensitive information and recommends additional safeguards.

22
00:02:00.739 --> 00:02:10.808
That is the honest summary for any redactor, model-based ones included: redaction lowers the amount of sensitive data in your logs; it does not bring it to zero.

23
00:02:11.088 --> 00:02:23.376
You can measure it, though, the same way you measured judges in Studio 3: label a few hundred real (access-controlled) log lines by hand, run the redactor, and report recall per entity type.

24
00:02:23.656 --> 00:02:28.051
Then decide whether the residual risk is acceptable for each destination.
